,{"type":"code","value":"// devtools-setup.tsx\nimport { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-devtools'\n\nexport default function Devtools(){\n  return <TanStackDevtools plugins={[\n    // Add your custom plugins here\n  ]} />\n}\n\n// App.tsx\nconst Devtools = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' ? await import('./devtools-setup') : () => null\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <YourApp />\n      <Devtools />\n    </>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"event-client-in-production","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event client in production"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"root import"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/devtools-event-client"},{"type":"text","value":" no-ops\noutside development. When your bundler sets "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"process.env.NODE_ENV"},{"type":"text","value":" to anything\nother than "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'development'"},{"type":"text","value":", the real client is replaced by a no-op and\ntree-shaken out of your production bundle:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// dev: real client — production: no-op (tree-shaken away)\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are an open-source author who deliberately wants devtools events in\nproduction, import the real client from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/production"},{"type":"text","value":" subpath instead. It is\nnever stripped:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// always the real client, in every environment\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client/production'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public API is identical between the two imports — only the production\nruntime behavior differs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is independent of the Vite plugin's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"removeDevtoolsOnBuild"},{"type":"text","value":" option — the event client strips itself based on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NODE_ENV"},{"type":"text","value":", whether or not you use the Vite plugin."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"webmcp-tools-in-production","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebMCP tools in production"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You search a production build for registered library WebMCP tools. The root import does not register them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With the root import, libraries register those tools only in development."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the tools must stay registered in production, import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { registerDevtoolsTools } from '@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production"},{"type":"text","value":" is always the real helper. The register call is the same as in "},{"type":"link","href":"./webmcp-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebMCP Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the root import for development."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { registerDevtoolsTools } from '@tanstack/devtools-webmcp'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"process.env.NODE_ENV"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'development'"},{"type":"text","value":", the root import is the real helper. In every other environment, the root import is a no-op. An unset "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NODE_ENV"},{"type":"text","value":" is a no-op too."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bundlers remove the real helper. The tool objects stay in the library bundle. The no-op does not call the browser. The no-op does not keep the tools object."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you build an app, stop the search. The root import does not register these tools in that bundle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-install-the-devtools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to install the Devtools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using the devtools in development only, you can install them as a development dependency and only import them in development builds. This is the default recommended way to use the devtools."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using the devtools in production, you need to install them as a regular dependency and import them in your application code."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This depends on if you are shedding development dependencies in production or not, but generally it's recommended to install them as a regular dependency if you are using them in production."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"development-production-workflow","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Development / Production workflow"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For Development, you can install the devtools as a development dependency and import them in your application code. The Vite plugin will take care of stripping them out of the production build."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For Production, you can install the devtools as a regular dependency and import them in your application code using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/production"},{"type":"text","value":" sub-export and then you also need to set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"removeDevtoolsOnBuild"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" in the Vite plugin configuration."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"vite-plugin-configuration","text":"Vite Plugin Configuration","level":2},{"id":"excluding-devtools-from-production-on-non-vite-projects","text":"Excluding Devtools from Production on non-vite projects","level":2},{"id":"event-client-in-production","text":"Event client in production","level":2},{"id":"webmcp-tools-in-production","text":"WebMCP tools in production","level":2},{"id":"where-to-install-the-devtools","text":"Where to install the Devtools","level":2},{"id":"development-production-workflow","text":"Development / Production workflow","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n\nThe whole point of devtools is to help you during development, so it's generally not recommended to include them in production builds, but if you know what you're doing, you can.\n\n\n## Vite Plugin Configuration\n\nThe default behavior of the Vite plugin is to remove all devtools related code from the production build. This is usually what you want, but if you want to keep the devtools in production (not recommended) you can set the `removeDevtoolsOnBuild` option to `false`.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      removeDevtoolsOnBuild: false\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\nThis will include the devtools and all it's plugins in the production build.\n\n## Excluding Devtools from Production on non-vite projects\n\nIf you're running devtools in a non-vite project you will have to manually exclude the devtools from your production build. You can do this by using environment variables or any other method you prefer.\n\nWe would recommend you create a separate file for the devtools import and then conditionally import that file in your main application file depending on the environment.\n\nHere's an example using environment variables:\n\n```tsx\n// devtools-setup.tsx\nimport { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-devtools'\n\nexport default function Devtools(){\n  return <TanStackDevtools plugins={[\n    // Add your custom plugins here\n  ]} />\n}\n\n// App.tsx\nconst Devtools = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' ? await import('./devtools-setup') : () => null\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <YourApp />\n      <Devtools />\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Event client in production\n\nBy default the **root import** of `@tanstack/devtools-event-client` no-ops\noutside development. When your bundler sets `process.env.NODE_ENV` to anything\nother than `'development'`, the real client is replaced by a no-op and\ntree-shaken out of your production bundle:\n\n```ts\n// dev: real client — production: no-op (tree-shaken away)\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'\n```\n\nIf you are an open-source author who deliberately wants devtools events in\nproduction, import the real client from the `/production` subpath instead. It is\nnever stripped:\n\n```ts\n// always the real client, in every environment\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client/production'\n```\n\nThe public API is identical between the two imports — only the production\nruntime behavior differs.\n\nThis is independent of the Vite plugin's `removeDevtoolsOnBuild` option — the event client strips itself based on `NODE_ENV`, whether or not you use the Vite plugin.\n\n## WebMCP tools in production\n\nYou search a production build for registered library WebMCP tools. The root import does not register them.\n\nWith the root import, libraries register those tools only in development.\n\nIf the tools must stay registered in production, import `@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production`.\n\n```ts\nimport { registerDevtoolsTools } from '@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production'\n```\n\nThe import `@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production` is always the real helper. The register call is the same as in [WebMCP Tools](./webmcp-tools).\n\nUse the root import for development.\n\n```ts\nimport { registerDevtoolsTools } from '@tanstack/devtools-webmcp'\n```\n\nWhen `process.env.NODE_ENV` is `'development'`, the root import is the real helper. In every other environment, the root import is a no-op. An unset `NODE_ENV` is a no-op too.\n\nBundlers remove the real helper. The tool objects stay in the library bundle. The no-op does not call the browser. The no-op does not keep the tools object.\n\nIf you build an app, stop the search. The root import does not register these tools in that bundle.\n\n## Where to install the Devtools\n\nIf you are using the devtools in development only, you can install them as a development dependency and only import them in development builds. This is the default recommended way to use the devtools.\n\nIf you are using the devtools in production, you need to install them as a regular dependency and import them in your application code.\n\nThis depends on if you are shedding development dependencies in production or not, but generally it's recommended to install them as a regular dependency if you are using them in production.\n\n## Development / Production workflow\n\nFor Development, you can install the devtools as a development dependency and import them in your application code. The Vite plugin will take care of stripping them out of the production build.\n\nFor Production, you can install the devtools as a regular dependency and import them in your application code using the `/production` sub-export and then you also need to set `removeDevtoolsOnBuild` to `false` in the Vite plugin configuration.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Production\nid: production\n---\n\n\nThe whole point of devtools is to help you during development, so it's generally not recommended to include them in production builds, but if you know what you're doing, you can.\n\n\n## Vite Plugin Configuration\n\nThe default behavior of the Vite plugin is to remove all devtools related code from the production build. This is usually what you want, but if you want to keep the devtools in production (not recommended) you can set the `removeDevtoolsOnBuild` option to `false`.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      removeDevtoolsOnBuild: false\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\nThis will include the devtools and all it's plugins in the production build.\n\n## Excluding Devtools from Production on non-vite projects\n\nIf you're running devtools in a non-vite project you will have to manually exclude the devtools from your production build. You can do this by using environment variables or any other method you prefer.\n\nWe would recommend you create a separate file for the devtools import and then conditionally import that file in your main application file depending on the environment.\n\nHere's an example using environment variables:\n\n```tsx\n// devtools-setup.tsx\nimport { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-devtools'\n\nexport default function Devtools(){\n  return <TanStackDevtools plugins={[\n    // Add your custom plugins here\n  ]} />\n}\n\n// App.tsx\nconst Devtools = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' ? await import('./devtools-setup') : () => null\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <YourApp />\n      <Devtools />\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Event client in production\n\nBy default the **root import** of `@tanstack/devtools-event-client` no-ops\noutside development. When your bundler sets `process.env.NODE_ENV` to anything\nother than `'development'`, the real client is replaced by a no-op and\ntree-shaken out of your production bundle:\n\n```ts\n// dev: real client — production: no-op (tree-shaken away)\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'\n```\n\nIf you are an open-source author who deliberately wants devtools events in\nproduction, import the real client from the `/production` subpath instead. It is\nnever stripped:\n\n```ts\n// always the real client, in every environment\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client/production'\n```\n\nThe public API is identical between the two imports — only the production\nruntime behavior differs.\n\nThis is independent of the Vite plugin's `removeDevtoolsOnBuild` option — the event client strips itself based on `NODE_ENV`, whether or not you use the Vite plugin.\n\n## WebMCP tools in production\n\nYou search a production build for registered library WebMCP tools. The root import does not register them.\n\nWith the root import, libraries register those tools only in development.\n\nIf the tools must stay registered in production, import `@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production`.\n\n```ts\nimport { registerDevtoolsTools } from '@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production'\n```\n\nThe import `@tanstack/devtools-webmcp/production` is always the real helper. The register call is the same as in [WebMCP Tools](./webmcp-tools).\n\nUse the root import for development.\n\n```ts\nimport { registerDevtoolsTools } from '@tanstack/devtools-webmcp'\n```\n\nWhen `process.env.NODE_ENV` is `'development'`, the root import is the real helper. In every other environment, the root import is a no-op. An unset `NODE_ENV` is a no-op too.\n\nBundlers remove the real helper. The tool objects stay in the library bundle. The no-op does not call the browser. The no-op does not keep the tools object.\n\nIf you build an app, stop the search. The root import does not register these tools in that bundle.\n\n## Where to install the Devtools\n\nIf you are using the devtools in development only, you can install them as a development dependency and only import them in development builds. This is the default recommended way to use the devtools.\n\nIf you are using the devtools in production, you need to install them as a regular dependency and import them in your application code.\n\nThis depends on if you are shedding development dependencies in production or not, but generally it's recommended to install them as a regular dependency if you are using them in production.\n\n## Development / Production workflow\n\nFor Development, you can install the devtools as a development dependency and import them in your application code. The Vite plugin will take care of stripping them out of the production build.\n\nFor Production, you can install the devtools as a regular dependency and import them in your application code using the `/production` sub-export and then you also need to set `removeDevtoolsOnBuild` to `false` in the Vite plugin configuration.\n"}
